 ## Controller gates and limits
 
 All product/test source was frozen after the covering passes. The controller has started a new full browser run at `/tmp/directors-release-browser.log`; this report does not predeclare its result. The earlier twelve-case canonical capture passed for `f72f858`, but must be regenerated for these visual/initial-load fixes. Canonical capture/metrics regeneration, asset-hash final checks, scoped independent re-review and preview review remain controller-owned.
 
 Touch-enabled WebKit captures/programmatic scrolling do not prove physical iOS swipes, Safari toolbar behavior, authored playback pacing, hardware/GPU smoothness or thermal performance. The new video regression repetitions establish correctness in the managed browser under the reported test concurrency, not a device-performance guarantee. Existing preview-only media rights/source limitations remain unchanged and block a production-readiness claim; no release safeguard was bypassed.
+
+## Reduced-motion completion of this same fix wave
+
+The next integrated run was **264 passed, 18 existing conditional skips, two failures**, so the wave was not treated as complete. Both failures were reduced-motion cases (`/tmp/directors-release-browser.log`): a real resize alignment race, and the new test incorrectly expecting motion-mode control hiding in a static reduced-motion document.
+
+Root diagnosis used actual managed Chromium reduced-motion contexts at 1280×720, immediately resized to 1280×600 before any Tab input. `/tmp/directors-reduced-resize-debug.mjs` reproduced three clipped states in six loads: scrollY stayed 720, the now-600px film stage began at −120, and the video began at −24 instead of below the 96px header. A separate `/tmp/directors-reduced-resize-confirm.mjs` run reproduced the same stale alignment and showed that an explicit resize redispatch immediately corrected scrollY to 600/stage top 0. The original strong resize test happened to pass four isolated repetitions; that did not override the full-run failure or this diagnostic.
+
+The window resize refresh can precede the settled viewport-dependent CSS geometry. The existing ResizeObserver watched galleries/statistics/milestones but not the film stage. The bounded product repair adds `#film-room` to that observer, so actual settled stage dimensions trigger alignment. The native-keyboard frame branch also now falls back to the film stage’s document position when reduced motion has no `arrival-cinema` ScrollTrigger, matching the existing direct-anchor behavior. No new delay was added and no framing bound was relaxed.
+
+The new native-key test now asserts the correct mode-specific public behavior after deliberate manual reverse scrolling: motion hides controls and sets tabIndex −1; reduced motion retains controls and tabIndex 0. It still checks the real scroll movement, focus, native readiness, framing, external `preventScroll` behavior and manual release. A private-token-presence check on the first ArrowRight failed once in managed WebKit while the actual controls/tabIndex remained true/0. Whether that was native event consumption or startup-listener timing was not independently established. That implementation-detail assertion was replaced with actual focused/ready/fully-visible player assertions; no event was forced and no test skipped. The original strong full Tab traversal and original direct-anchor framing assertions remain unchanged.
+
+A new current-source reduced-motion fixture changes both preceding-layout and film height after the window resize alignment frame, then verifies the actual stage/video return to the same pixel positions. An initial WebKit fixture run exposed an omitted mobile viewport meta tag (1.333px zoom rounding); adding the app-equivalent viewport meta corrected the fixture without loosening the pixel assertions.
+
+Actual verification after the controller’s settled-layout build:
+
+- `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_VALIDATE_HOST_REQUIREMENTS=1 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/zk17-libs/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu npx playwright test tests/e2e/story-focus.spec.ts --grep 'reduced film anchor' --project=desktop-chromium --project=iphone-webkit --workers=1 --output=/tmp/directors-reduced-layout-source-final`: **2/2 passed**. Log `/tmp/directors-reduced-layout-source-final.log`.
+- `node --input-type=module < /tmp/directors-reduced-resize-debug.mjs > /tmp/directors-reduced-resize-fixed.log 2>&1`: **six of six** fast reduced-motion loads/resizes had scrollY 600, stage top 0, video top 96 and native tabIndex 0, with no Tab input.
+- First complete cinema run after the product patch: 33 passed, two existing conditional skips and one private-token assertion failure described above; retained at `/tmp/directors-reduced-completion-cinema.log`.
+- Final observable-behavior coverage: `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_VALIDATE_HOST_REQUIREMENTS=1 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/zk17-libs/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu npx playwright test tests/e2e/cinema-framing.spec.ts --workers=2 --output=/tmp/directors-reduced-completion-cinema-final`: **34 passed, two existing desktop-layout skips, zero failures**, across all four configured projects. Log `/tmp/directors-reduced-completion-cinema-final.log`. This includes preserved native Tab traversal, short-player focus, forward/reverse aperture, both resize guarantees, mode-correct manual release and Playoffs placement.
+- Scoped ESLint and TypeScript passed; the controller also reported 56 unit tests/full lint/typecheck passing on the settled-layout candidate. Owned-file diff whitespace check passed before the amendment commit.
+
+Product/test source was frozen after these passes. The controller’s fresh full run is now `/tmp/directors-complete-browser.log`; its result, final captures/metrics and the scoped final re-review remain controller-owned and are not predeclared here. The prior eight-repeat WebKit evidence remains historical for the first wave candidate; the latest six-line product completion is covered by the complete four-project cinema run above.
